Ralph approves

Sample heartbeat logic

Use heartbeat for the decision, not the heavy work.

A HEARTBEAT.md concept like this is the right shape. OpenClaw docs note that HEARTBEAT.md can be empty to skip heartbeat API calls, or contain periodic tasks to check.

# HEARTBEAT

1. Check for urgent user, inbox, calendar, or system tasks.
2. If urgent work exists, do not train.
3. If no urgent work exists:
   - check queue depth
   - check system headroom
   - check training quota
4. If all gates pass:
   - enqueue one training task from TRAINING.md or curriculum/*
   - prefer reflection/postmortem before external content
5. Never during heartbeat:
   - install a new skill
   - modify production configs
   - enable new tools
   - spend cloud budget beyond quota
6. If nothing needs attention, reply HEARTBEAT_OK.
Sample cron pattern

Use cron for isolated heavy training:

nightly Scout research training

nightly Charlie coding-pattern training

weekly Quill content teardown

weekly Ralph red-team review

The docs’ guidance is straightforward: heartbeat for batched periodic checks; cron for exact schedules, one-shot reminders, or isolated runs.

Conceptually:

openclaw cron add --name "Scout nightly training" --cron "0 1 * * *" --session isolated --message "Run one approved training item from curriculum/scout.md. Use YouTube Vision if needed. Produce summary, extracted tactics, candidate checklist, and eval notes. Do not change production." --announce
The training curriculum order

This is the order I’d use:

Reflection first

review failures

inspect bad outputs

improve prompts/checklists

Internal corpus second

your docs

your transcripts

your past runs

your approved patterns

External text/web third

articles

docs

changelogs

discussions

YouTube fourth

only for topics where video adds real value

That order is important. Most agents do not need more “content.” They need better memory, better routing, and fewer stupid mistakes.

The visual / fun layer

You wanted funny and useful. Good. Do both.

In the 2D world, training states can be obvious and lightweight:

Scout in a “research library”

Charlie in a “dojo”

Quill in a “writer’s room”

Ralph in a “test chamber”

Todd in a “war room”

Tiny overlays:

book icon = internal doc study

TV icon = YouTube training

wrench icon = skill candidate being built

shield icon = Ralph evaluating

trophy/trash icons = promoted or rejected

That makes the system legible without wasting real compute on a giant game engine.

What not to do

Don’t let agents:

auto-install skills from what they watched

edit openclaw.json during training

change tool permissions during training

browse production credentials while “learning”

treat every watched video as ground truth

use the same model for training, grading, and promotion

That last one matters. Self-grading is how bullshit becomes policy.

